# Worked Example: Router Integration

Toolcrib has zero router awareness by design — no React Router, TanStack
Router, or Next navigation import anywhere in the toolkit. This file walks
through the points where a real router actually meets toolcrib: provider
nesting order, controlled overlays for deep-linking, `<TabStrip>`/URL
sync, and imperative navigation from anywhere via the event bus.

## Where `<ToolcribProvider>` sits relative to your router

`useTheme()`/`useToast()` throw `"must be used within a ...Provider"` the
moment a component using them renders outside that provider's subtree —
ordinary React Context, no toolcrib-specific magic. The practical
consequence for routing: `<ToolcribProvider>` must wrap your router's
routes, not sit inside one layout route among several — a route that
doesn't happen to render under it will throw the moment it uses
`useToast()` or any themed component. Put it at the true render root,
above `<BrowserRouter>`/`<RouterProvider>`/Next's root layout, once.

## Controlled vs. uncontrolled overlays

`<Modal>`, `<Drawer>`, and `<Popup>` all default to **uncontrolled**:
`aiBus.openModal(id)` / `aiBus.closeModal(id)` toggle them without any
`isOpen` prop at all — the component owns its own open/closed state
internally. For URL-driven deep-linking, pass the controlled `isOpen`
prop instead, deriving it from route state so the overlay is **controlled**:
the parent owns the state and the component only ever reflects it.

```tsx
import { Modal } from '#toolcrib';
import { useParams } from 'react-router-dom';

function ItemDetailModal() {
  const { itemId } = useParams();
  return (
    <Modal id="item-detail" isOpen={Boolean(itemId)} onOpenChange={(open) => {
      if (!open) history.back();
    }}>
      <Modal.Body>{/* ... */}</Modal.Body>
    </Modal>
  );
}
```

`onOpenChange` fires for both the URL-driven case and any internal close
(Escape key, backdrop click) — reconcile the URL there rather than race
it against the bus.

## Two-way `<TabStrip>` / URL sync

`<TabStrip>`'s `onChange` prop exists specifically "for syncing with a
router" (its own JSDoc says so) — pass a controlled `activeId` derived
from the URL, and update the URL from `onChange`:

```tsx
<TabStrip
  id="settings-tabs"
  items={tabs}
  activeId={searchParams.get('tab') ?? 'general'}
  onChange={(id) => setSearchParams({ tab: id })}
/>
```

`<TabStrip.Panel groupId="settings-tabs">` instances elsewhere in the tree
don't need any of this — they read `tab:changed` off the bus regardless
of whether `<TabStrip>` is controlled or self-managed (see the
event-bus-sticky-replay example for why that channel is sticky).

## Imperative navigation from anywhere: `RouterAdapterProvider` + `aiBus.navigate()`

For the case with no natural "controlled prop" — a `CommandPalette` item,
a toast action, a modal's confirm handler — wire up the bridge once,
inside your router's own tree (wherever the real navigate function
exists):

```tsx
import { RouterAdapterProvider, useRouterBridge } from '#toolcrib';
import { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom';

function RouterBridgeMount() {
  useRouterBridge();
  return null;
}

function AppRoutes() {
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  return (
    <RouterAdapterProvider adapter=\{{ navigate }}>
      <RouterBridgeMount />
      {/* routes */}
    </RouterAdapterProvider>
  );
}

// Root: <ToolcribProvider><BrowserRouter><AppRoutes /></BrowserRouter></ToolcribProvider>
```

Then, from anywhere — a `CommandPalette` `onSelect`, a toast action's
`onClick` — with no shared ancestor with the router required:

```ts
aiBus.navigate('/settings/billing');
```

`route:navigate`'s payload is:

```ts
{{routeNavigatePayload}}
```

Deliberately **not** a sticky event: it's a one-shot imperative command,
not state a late subscriber should replay. A `RouterAdapterProvider`/
`useRouterBridge()` pair mounted *after* `aiBus.navigate()` already fired
will not retroactively navigate.

The adapter object is opaque to *how* your router actually navigates —
the same `{ navigate }` from React Router's `useNavigate()` works
identically whether the app uses `<BrowserRouter>` (History API) or
`<HashRouter>` (`#/path` URLs). Without a router library at all, a manual
hash adapter works the same way:

```ts
<RouterAdapterProvider adapter=\{{ navigate: (to) => { window.location.hash = to; } }}>
```

This is worth calling out explicitly, not just as a fallback: toolcrib's
own real sample apps (Feed Farmer, Founder's Desk) both already dropped
`react-router` in favor of plain view-state switching, and a hash-based
scheme — needed for GitHub Pages deep-link support, since Pages has no
server-side rewrite for History-API routing — is a more likely next step
for an app in that same minimalist style than adopting a router library.

If `useRouterBridge()` is mounted with no `RouterAdapterProvider` above
it — no router configured at all — it's a safe no-op with a dev-only
console warning the moment a navigation is actually attempted, not a hard
throw: unlike `useTheme()`/`useToast()`, mounting this hook isn't required
by every toolcrib consumer, so nothing should force a router-less app to
avoid it defensively.

## Reconstructing state after back/forward navigation

This is not a toolcrib mechanism. A back/forward navigation is handled
entirely by the router (or a raw `hashchange` listener) re-rendering with
new state — `aiBus`/`RouterAdapterProvider` are never involved, since that
channel only carries the opposite direction (component → router).
Controlled props (`Modal`'s `isOpen`, `TabStrip`'s `activeId`) reconstruct
correctly for free *only if* they're derived fresh from the router's live
state on every render — `isOpen={Boolean(useParams().itemId)}`, or
`useSyncExternalStore` over `window.location.hash` for the no-library
case — not from local state only ever updated inside an event handler.
The idiomatic uncontrolled path (`aiBus.openModal(id)`) cannot participate
in this at all: if deep-linking or back-nav needs to affect a given
overlay, controlled `isOpen` is mandatory for it, not optional.

Deriving state from the URL is only the *read* half, though. See the next
section for the missing *write* half — reading a URL that nothing ever
wrote reconstructs nothing.

## Worked scenario: a drawer's "view original" link, and why Back loses it

Concrete case, in the shape of a feed reader: a `<Drawer>` opens showing
an article — today, via plain `aiBus.openDrawer(id)`, uncontrolled,
in-memory only — and its "view original" button navigates same-tab to
the article's source page. Clicking browser Back afterward lands on the
app's root, not back on that article's drawer, because nothing ever
created a history entry for "drawer open on article X" in the first
place. Deriving `isOpen` from the URL, as above, has nothing to read,
since nothing wrote it.

Two fixes, in order of preference:

- **Default: open the external link in a new tab**
  (`target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"`) instead of a same-tab
  navigation. This is the standard pattern for a "view original source"
  action specifically because it sidesteps the problem entirely — the
  SPA's own tab/history is never touched, so there's nothing for Back to
  get wrong. Reach for this before the pattern below.
- **When a same-tab navigation away genuinely needs to survive a
  subsequent Back**: *opening* the drawer must itself push a history
  entry (a hash change or `history.pushState`) at open time, not just
  flip in-memory state. Read-from-URL and write-to-URL-on-open are a
  pair — deriving `isOpen` from the current hash on mount/`popstate` only
  works if something wrote that hash when the drawer opened to begin
  with.
